/-!
# The MST 2-approximation for metric Steiner tree

In the Steiner tree problem we must connect a set `R` of *terminals* as cheaply as possible, and
may route through other points (*Steiner points*). This module proves that a minimum spanning tree
on the terminals alone costs at most twice the optimum when the cost is symmetric and satisfies the
triangle inequality ([WilliamsonShmoys2011], Exercise 2.5(a)). Williamson and Shmoys attribute the
factor `2` to Moore, as described in [GilbertPollak1968] ([WilliamsonShmoys2011], Chapter 12
notes). We prove the sharper form `|R| · MST ≤ 2 (|R| - 1) · OPT`.

## Model

Points have an arbitrary type `α` and a cost `δ : α → α → ℕ`. An edge set is a list of ordered
pairs `T : List (α × α)` with additive cost `edgeCost δ T`. `Connects T R` says that the equivalence
relation generated by the edges of `T` relates every two terminals. The edges may use any points of
`α`, so `T` may pass through Steiner points. `steinerOpt δ R` is the least cost of a list that
connects `R`. Costs are in `ℕ`, hence nonnegative, so this is also the least cost of a tree that
connects `R`: dropping edges that close cycles keeps connectivity and does not increase the cost.

The algorithm `steinerMST δ R` takes a duplicate-free list of terminals `R`. It runs Kruskal's
algorithm (`Cslib.Algorithms.Lean.Kruskal.kruskalEdges`) on the complete graph on the indices
`Fin R.length` with costs `terminalCost δ R i j = δ R[i] R[j]`, and maps the resulting tree back to
the terminals. This is the minimum spanning tree of the subgraph `G[R]` induced by the terminals in
the source. The bounds need `R.Nodup` because `δ x x` may be positive. With `δ x x = 5`, the
heuristic pays `5` to join the two copies in `R = [1, 1]`, while `steinerOpt δ [1, 1] = 0` (see
`CslibFrontierTests.SteinerTree`). When the diagonal cost is zero (`∀ x, δ x x = 0`), repeated
terminals do not increase the cycle cost, and the bounds hold for arbitrary terminal lists
(`length_mul_edgeCost_steinerMST_le_of_diag`).

## Source mapping

* [WilliamsonShmoys2011], Exercise 2.5(a): for costs that satisfy the triangle inequality, a
  minimum spanning tree of `G[R]` is a 2-approximation for Steiner tree. The Lean statement is
  `edgeCost_steinerMST_le_two_mul_steinerOpt`. The exercise's complete graph has symmetric costs
  `c_ij`, which is the hypothesis `hsymm`. The exercise asks only for factor `2`.
* `length_mul_edgeCost_steinerMST_le` is the sharper bound `2 (1 - 1/|R|)`. Kou, Markowsky and
  Berman [KouMarkowskyBerman1981] prove the still sharper `2 (1 - 1/ℓ)`, where `ℓ ≤ |R|` is the
  number of leaves of an optimal tree. That leaf bound is **not** formalized here.
* The graph version (the metric completion and Kou–Markowsky–Berman's expansion of the tree into
  shortest paths, [WilliamsonShmoys2011], Exercise 2.5(b)) is in
  `CslibFrontier.Algorithms.Lean.SteinerTree.Graph`.

## Proof

Restrict a connecting list `T` to the component that contains the terminals, then double each
edge. Every vertex now has even degree, so Hierholzer's algorithm
(`Cslib.Algorithms.Lean.Euler.eulerCircuit`) gives an Euler circuit of cost `2 · cost(T)`.
Shortcutting it to the terminals (`Cslib.Algorithms.Lean.TSP.cycleCost_le_of_sublist`) gives a
Hamiltonian cycle on `R` of cost at most `2 · cost(T)`. Deleting its most expensive edge leaves a
Hamiltonian path that costs at most `(1 - 1/|R|)` of the cycle, and the path weighs at least the
minimum spanning tree (`mul_totalWeight_kruskalEdges_le`).

## Main definitions

* `Connects`, `edgeCost`: feasibility and cost of an edge list.
* `terminalCost`, `steinerMST`: the MST heuristic on the terminals.
* `steinerOpt`: the optimum Steiner cost.

## Main results

* `totalWeight_kruskalEdges_le_sum_consecutivePairs`: Kruskal's tree on a complete graph weighs at
  most any Hamiltonian path (through `TSP.totalWeight_kruskalEdges_completeEdges_le`).
* `mul_totalWeight_kruskalEdges_le`: `k · MST ≤ (k - 1) · cost(C)` for every Hamiltonian cycle `C`
  on `k` vertices. This sharpens [WilliamsonShmoys2011], Lemma 2.10, which states `MST ≤ cost(C)`.
* `connects_steinerMST`: the heuristic connects the terminals.
* `length_mul_edgeCost_steinerMST_le`: `|R| · cost(steinerMST) ≤ 2 (|R| - 1) · cost(T)` for every
  list `T` that connects `R`, assuming `R.Nodup`.
* `length_mul_edgeCost_steinerMST_le_of_diag`: `|R| · cost(steinerMST) ≤ 2 (|R| - 1) · cost(T)`
  when `∀ x, δ x x = 0`, without assuming `R.Nodup`.
* `edgeCost_steinerMST_le_two_mul`, `edgeCost_steinerMST_le_two_mul_steinerOpt`,
  `length_mul_edgeCost_steinerMST_le_steinerOpt`: approximation bounds for duplicate-free terminals.
* `edgeCost_steinerMST_le_two_mul_of_diag`, `edgeCost_steinerMST_le_two_mul_steinerOpt_of_diag`,
  `length_mul_edgeCost_steinerMST_le_steinerOpt_of_diag`: the zero-diagonal approximation bounds
  for arbitrary terminal lists.

## Limitations

The statements are about the output and the optimum, not about running time. No time bound for
`steinerMST` is stated here.

## References

* [D. P. Williamson and D. B. Shmoys, *The Design of Approximation
  Algorithms*][WilliamsonShmoys2011], Exercise 2.5 and Chapter 12 notes.
* [E. N. Gilbert and H. O. Pollak, *Steiner minimal trees*][GilbertPollak1968]
* [L. Kou, G. Markowsky and L. Berman, *A fast algorithm for Steiner trees*][KouMarkowskyBerman1981]
-/

